feat: back the song list screens with the server's playlists

lx's song list browsing UI (playlist grid plus detail, with play-all,
download and batch actions) reads its data through the source layer's
songList interface, and core/songlist.ts uses only three of its methods:
getTags, getList and getListDetail. Implementing those three makes the
whole existing UI work without touching a single screen, which is far
more reliable than rewriting it and gives play-all for free.

Mapping, since the semantics differ from a commercial source: getList
becomes getAllUserLists, getListDetail becomes getListMusics, and getTags
returns nothing because any-listen has no playlist categories. sortList
keeps a single entry so the UI does not render sort buttons that do
nothing.

Two assumptions were wrong and were corrected against the live server:

- getAllUserLists returns {defaultList, loveList, lastPlayList,
  userList}, not an array, so reading .list returned an empty grid every
  time. The real playlists are in userList.
- The list meta has no cover, author or time fields at all. It does have
  songCount, path and enabledRemove.

enabledRemove turns out to be the natural marker for which lists belong
in a browser: the server's mounted folder ("all songs", type local) has
it false while user-created playlists have it true, so the grid shows
the lists a user can actually manage.

getListDetail returns search-item shaped entries rather than converted
MusicInfo, matching what every upstream source does. core/songlist.ts:111
runs toNewMusicInfo over each entry and that function reads songmid, img,
types and _types and dereferences meta._qualitys unconditionally, so
handing it an already-converted object throws on every track. This also
carries meta.anylisten through toNewMusicInfo's anylisten branch, which
is what getMusicUrl depends on.

Paging is local because the server returns whole playlists with no paging
parameter; getListDetail's cached fetch keeps that from re-requesting
1653 tracks on every page.
